The upstream project ships the same rules in its tool guard
(dangerous_shell_commands.yaml). Jonathan Ai uses that rule set to hold
destructive commands, disk wipes, fork bombs, curl-or-wget piped into a
shell, credential exfiltration, and the other bundled patterns until the
user approves them. Approvals are on by default.

Jonathan Ai also masks API keys, tokens, passwords, and private keys in
tool output and logs, following Octop's secret-masking approach. Personal
identity number detection from the upstream detector is not included.
No Jonathan Ai code in this port calls a third-party hosted endpoint.

Jonathan Ai long-term memory is adapted from Octop's local SQLite memory
store. Facts are extracted from chats and can be recalled later. The
database file stays under the user's home directory and each row is scoped
to that user. Jonathan Ai uses the Python stdlib sqlite3 module for this
store. It does not vendor Octop's memory graph, vector index, or networked
extractor, and it does not add a third-party package. Secrets are masked
before they are written. Users can list and delete their own memories.

